quotes": [{"text": "Now torne we to the xj kynges that retorned vnto a cyte that hyghte Sorhaute / the whiche cyte was within kynge Vryens / and ther they refresshed hem as wel as they myght / and made leches serche theyr woundys and sorowed gretely for the dethe of her peple", "reference": "1485, Sir Thomas Malory, “xvj”, in Le Morte Darthur, book I:"}, {"text": "Now to the bottome dost thou search my wound.", "reference": "c. 1588–1593 (date written), William Shakespeare, “The Lamentable Tragedy of Titus Andronicus”, in Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies […] (First Folio), London: […] Isaac Iaggard, and Ed[ward] Blount, published 1623, →OCLC, [Act II, scene iii]:"}, {"text": "Thus when they all had sorowed their fill, / They softly gan to search his griesly wownd[…].", "reference": "1590, Edmund Spenser, “Book III, Canto IV”, in The Faerie Queene. […], London: […] [John Wolfe] for William Ponsonbie, →OCLC:"}, {"text": "His wife perceiving him to droope and languish away, entreated him she might leasurely search and neerely view the quality of his disease[…].", "reference": "1603, Michel de Montaigne, chapter 35, in John Florio, transl., The Essayes […], book II, London: […] Val[entine] Simmes for Edward Blount […], →OCLC:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) To examine; to try; to put to the test.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": ["comb", "scour", "look for", "seek", "comb"], "antonyms": []}], "source": {"url": "https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/search", "license": {"name": "CC BY-SA 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/"}}}}